  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{-4}{2-6\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$",
  "worked_solution": "The denominator is $2-6\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $2+6\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $2+6\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-4}{2-6\\text{i}} & =\\frac{-4}{2-6\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{2+6\\text{i}}{2+6\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8-24\\text{i}}{4+12\\text{i}-12\\text{i}-36{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8-24\\text{i}}{4-36{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8-24\\text{i}}{4-(36)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8-24\\text{i}}{4+36} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8-24\\text{i}}{40}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-8-24\\text{i}}{40}=-\\frac{8}{40}-\\frac{24}{40}\\text{i}=(-\\frac{1}{5})+(-\\frac{3}{5})\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
